Note = {1st International Conference on Sustainable Urbanization (ICSU), Hong
   Kong, PEOPLES R CHINA, DEC 15-17, 2010},
Organization = {Hong Kong Polytechn Univ, Fac Construct \& Land Use; Inst Urban
   Environm; Road King Infrastructure Ltd; Sun Hung Kai Properties; Chun Wo
   Dev Holdings Ltd; K C Wong Educ Fdn; Environm \& Conservat Fund; Gammon;
   Paul Y; Architecture Design \& Res Grp Ltd (AD+RG); China State
   Construct (CSCEC); Sino Grp; Vantage Int (Holdings) Ltd; ASCE Hong Kong
   Sect; ASHRAE Hong Kong Chapter; CIBSE; Construct Ind Council (CIC);
   Chartered Inst Bldg (CIOB); Council Sustainable Dev; Emerald; Hong Kong
   Inst Architects; Hong Kong Institut Engineers; Hong Kong Inst Surveyors;
   HKSTS; IUE; JSCE; RICS; Urban Planning Soc China; China Civil Engn Soc},
Abstract = {There is an increasing world population and urban regeneration and
   development. The concept `Sustainable Cities' is seductive to both
   practice and research, which however does not lead to any
   universally-agreed definition. Such a case is further complicated by the
   emergence of the many similar concepts like Eco-towns, Sustainable
   Communities, Self-sufficient Communities, and EcoDensity, as well as a
   number of ranking systems attempting to address sustainability in urban
   contexts. This paper examines the theories underlying the concepts of
   sustainability and sustainable cities, with the purpose to clarify the
   knowledge of the long term sustainable urban development. The paper then
   scrutinises the theoretical basis of the ranking systems for sustainable
   cities and their underlying methodology and measurement criteria and
   indicators. The systems examined include the UK Sustainable Cities
   Index, the SustainLane's US City Ranking, the Canadian Sustainable
   Cities, and the European Smart Cities. All these systems share the
   environmental, social and economic triangle ideology and reflect the
   relative and value-laden nature of sustainability. The ranking
   methodologies indicate the common use of a convenience sampling strategy
   for identifying measurement indicators and collecting data. Six key
   issues are raised in relation to the ranking framework, cities,
   indicators, weighting, data and ranking results, which need to be
   addressed if forward thinking is to flourish and sustainable cities
   rankings do not end up as a pure publicity show.},
