Year = {1999},
Volume = {3},
Pages = {435-440},
Note = {XIVth Annual International Occupational Ergonomics and Safety Conference
   1999, ORLANDO, FL, JUN 06-09, 1999},
Abstract = {This paper analyzes the ergonomic impact of eye depth perception in a
   work environment. The analysis uses a patented eye depth perception
   system that was designed and developed by the author and is capable of
   accurately measuring eye depth perception under various environmental
   conditions. One hundred and fifty subjects were tested under different
   environmental conditions (e.g., light intensity, color,: shape, viewing
   angle, distance between objects, and distance from a source). The study
   concluded that eye depth perception is directly related to various
   ergonomic factors, including: (1) object colors. (2) light intensity,
   (3) distances from objects, and (4) distances from a source.
   This study also determined that eye depth perception is an important
   factor in industrial accidents and employee well being. The author
   attempts to help modern ergonomists to become more aware of ergonomic
   problems in a work environment and to better understand depth perception
   as a scientific criterion in ergonomic evaluation of today's safety
   problems.},
