   Wolverhampton, Wolverhampton, ENGLAND, MAY 09-12, 1999},
Organization = {Univ Wolverhampton, Sch Appl Sci; Univ Wolverhampton, Sch Engn; Univ
   Wolverhampton, Built Environm},
Abstract = {The development of many urban areas in the UK was associated with the
   Industrial Revolution and the use of fossil fuels. Unfortunately, both
   domestic and industrial use of coal led to severe atmospheric pollution,
   resulting in soiling and deterioration of natural stone buildings.
   During the first half of the 20th century, pollution due to the
   combustion of coal shifted from industry to the generation of
   electricity in power stations and domestic fires. As a result of the
   severe pollution in the 1950s, clean air legislation and new
   technological methods were introduced to reduce pollution from coal.
   Now, urban pollution is dominated by traffic-related pollution from
   petrol and diesel vehicles. The nature of coal and diesel particulate
   have been reviewed, with special reference to the deterioration of
   building stone.},
