Note = {22nd Congress of the International Ergonomics Association-IEA, SOUTH
   KOREA, AUG 25-29, 2024},
Abstract = {The SARS-CoV-2 pandemic, responsible for the COVID-19 disease, has led
   to an increase in the number of people working from home and the amount
   of time spent on remote work activities. Hence, it becomes imperative to
   enhance the thermal and environmental comfort, given the heightened
   frequency of computer screen usage, compounded by the rise in heat
   sources within the environment. However, over the last few decades,
   there has been an increase in understanding modern sources of lower
   frequency radiation in and around building complexes, and their effects
   on inhabitants and their surroundings. Non-ionizing radiation (NIR)
   includes a variety of low-frequency electromagnetic radiation that is
   generated by a variety of sources, such as household appliances, Wi-Fi
   systems, cell phones, and other electronic devices. Additionally, urban
   morphology (UM) may also play a role in radiation exposure depending on
   factors such as population density, building distribution, and
   communications infrastructure. Thus, this article presents a preliminary
   study observing and analyzing aspects of the UM (surroundings), thermal
   characteristics and NIR levels in 13 home offices located in heat
   islands in the city of Joao Pessoa, Paraiba, Brazil. The results
   demonstrate that all home offices presented temperatures and NIR levels
   above the recommended levels established by current standards and
   literature. Furthermore, in home offices located in heat islands, the
   combination of total built density, the roughness and height of tall
   buildings, and a low sky view factor provides evidence of thermal
   discomfort and elevated NIR intensity levels in these areas.},
