@inproceedings{ WOS:000188992200006,
Author = {Claval, P},
Editor = {Ehlers, E and Gethmann, CF},
Title = {Ethics and nature. A contextual approach},
Booktitle = {ENVIRONMENT ACROSS CULTURES},
Series = {Wissenschaftsethik und Technikfolgenbeurteilung},
Year = {2003},
Volume = {19},
Pages = {75-89},
Note = {Scientific Conference on Environment Across Cultures, BAD
   NEUENAHR-AHRWEILER, GERMANY, SEP 21-23, 1999},
Organization = {Human Dimens Programme Global Change; Europai Akad Erforsch
   Wissenschaftlich Techn},
Abstract = {Ethics and nature can be approached from different perspectives: in this
   book Harold Coward and Dieter Birnbacher offer excellent and thought
   provoking reviews of the contributions of philosophy and the history of
   religions to that field. I am not an historian of theology or a
   philosopher. I am a geographer, a geographer interested in the cultural
   approach to the discipline(46) : I think that the best I can do is to
   present, as a counterpoint to Coward's and Birnbacher's papers, a
   geographical perspective on the problem.
   Geographers have developed a strong interest in man/milieu relationship
   since the time of George Perkins Marsh (1864), Elisee Reclus (1969) and
   Friedrich Ratzel (1882-1891). The exploration of man/milieu
   relationships went a step further with Eduard Hahn (1896; 1909; 1914),
   who stressed the fundamental difference between plough and hoe
   cultivation, and the role of domesticated animals. For the majority of
   German geographers, it was through landscape studies that the problems
   of environment were dealt with (Storkebaum, 1967). In the United States,
   the position of Carl O. Sauer (1962) was in a way similar. Later on,
   geographers became influential in the development of a new consciousness
   of environmental problems, thanks to the stimulating congress on Man
   role in changing the face of the Earth, organized by William L. Thomas
   (195 6). Clarence Glacken's Traces on the Rhodian Shore (1967) offered a
   fascinating account of the main philosophical conceptions of nature in
   Western thought from the Greeks to the late eighteenth century. French
   contribution to this general movement came mainly from an approach
   developed by Vidal de la Blache (1922): in order to understand the
   relations between human groups and their environment, he analyzed the
   techniques, behaviours and attitudes used to extract from the milieu
   what was necessary to live; his central concept was that of genre de
   vie. The main contribution of geographers to the development of
   environmental consciousness was to stress the significance of scale in
   environmental problems and attitudes.
   The cultural approach relies on an interpretative grid which
   distinguishes three levels of analysis: the material techniques on which
   life is founded, the social techniques used to control collective action
   and the representations which provide the models and values which
   motivate people (Claval, 1995; 1999). My presentation is based on that
   grid: 1. I shall first consider the conditions in which the prevailing
   techniques generate environmental problems in traditional pre-industrial
   societies on the one hand, and modern industrialised and
   post-industrialised societies on the other. Technical evolution was
   responsible for major changes in the scale of environmental problems,
   with important consequences on the responsibilities of low and high
   cultures concerning the environment. 2. My second point will cover the
   relations between environmental values and the paths used to resolve
   environmental problems: personal values are not enough to succeed;
   questions of social and political organisation and collective ethics are
   involved. 3. The third contribution of the cultural approach is to focus
   on the experience of decentring upon which the building of values rely,
   and which explains the variety of ethical systems.
   My aim is to show that: 1. the relations between ethics and nature
   changed of scale with modernisation, which gave scientists new
   responsibilities; 2. natural problems are always at the same time social
   problems, which means that environmental ethics have to be adapted to
   specific political and social contexts; 3. the responsibility of
   scientists is high, since their reflections are used to fuel new
   ideologies: they cannot ignore this dimension of their enterprise.},
