   Buildings/3rd International Conference on Built Environment and Public
   Health, Guilin, PEOPLES R CHINA, MAY 29-31, 2009},
Organization = {Hunan Univ; Univ Hong Kong; Tsinghua Univ},
Abstract = {Particle number concentrations were measured continuously for three days
   indoors and Outdoors of an urban Californian home, using water-based
   condensational particle counters (TSI Model 3781). The acquired data
   were used to calculate the total particle inhalation and inhalation
   intake rate of the residents for the monitored periods. Their inhalation
   intake was apportioned among four contributing source categories:
   outdoor, constant indoor, episodic indoor, and unknown. The average
   indoor and outdoor particle number concentrations during this period
   were 2.25 x 10(4) cm(-3) and 1.48 x 10(4) cm(-3), respectively. The
   indoor concentration increased above the Outdoor concentration by an
   order of magnitude during cooking events. Fifty to seventy percent of
   the resident's particle intake was attributed to the cooking source
   category, and most of the remainder was attributed to the outdoor and
   constant-indoor categories. These results indicate that indoor sources
   contribute significantly to all individual's ultrafine particle
   exposure.},
